<h6 id=3D"multi-head-attention">Multi-Head Attention</h6>
<p>To capture different types of relationships and contextual dependencies,=
 the self-attention mechanism is often extended to multi-head attention. In=
stead of computing a single set of <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"kat=
ex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><m=
row><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">Q</annotati=
on></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true=
"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.8778em; ver=
tical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">Q</span></s=
pan></span></span>, <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><mat=
h xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><=
/mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics>=
</math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=
=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.6833em;"></span><span cl=
ass=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.07153em;">K</span></span><=
/span></span>, and <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mathml"><math=
 xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></=
mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics><=
/math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D=
"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class=
=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.22222em;">V</span></span></sp=
an></span> vectors, the input is projected into multiple sets, or =E2=80=9C=
heads,=E2=80=9D each with its own learnable weight matrices:</p>
<span class=3D"katex-display"><span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-ma=
thml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display=3D"block">=
